WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.280 --> 00:00:01.760
Books inspire us.

00:00:01.840 --> 00:00:02.960
They teach us.

00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.240
They let us escape into new worlds.

00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:08.000
But some books
do much more than this.

00:00:08.440 --> 00:00:10.520
These books hit a collective nerve,

00:00:10.600 --> 00:00:13.680
and have had a profound
and lasting effect on society.

00:00:14.600 --> 00:00:16.680
What are the titles
that had this impact?

00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:19.760
There are many, many books
that fit this definition,

00:00:20.240 --> 00:00:22.920
but we've managed to narrow
it down to five books

00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:25.040
that have truly shaped our world.

00:00:30.440 --> 00:00:33.840
...wrote Robert Burton in his
enormous 900 page book,

00:00:33.920 --> 00:00:35.680
The Anatomy of Melancholy.

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:39.160
Which was first published
way back in 1621.

00:00:39.880 --> 00:00:41.520
So in the 17th Century,

00:00:41.600 --> 00:00:45.000
there weren't books
about melancholy,

00:00:45.080 --> 00:00:47.640
or as we would call it today,
depression.

00:00:47.720 --> 00:00:51.320
It looks as if Robert Burton
himself suffered from depression.

00:00:51.400 --> 00:00:52.205
And there's a real sense

00:00:52.205 --> 00:00:56.640
as he writes it, that he's writing himself into a position

00:00:56.920 --> 00:01:00.560
of expertise about depression, so he can help himself

00:01:00.640 --> 00:01:02.040
as well as other people.

00:01:02.120 --> 00:01:05.640
And it's remained such a
kind of influential book

00:01:05.720 --> 00:01:07.120
over the centuries since then.

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:09.720
Not simply because
of its subject matter,

00:01:09.800 --> 00:01:13.360
it's the way it's written -
it explores inner life

00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:17.000
in a way that hadn't really
been explored before.

00:01:17.080 --> 00:01:18.760
So it's the patient's voice.

00:01:19.080 --> 00:01:21.520
And one of the things that we've
learnt over the last 20 years

00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:24.240
is the importance
of the patient's voice,

00:01:24.320 --> 00:01:27.720
in targeting research
and clinical treatment.

00:01:27.800 --> 00:01:29.560
To 1960s America now,

00:01:29.640 --> 00:01:33.280
where many people still thought
that a woman's place was in the home.

00:01:33.360 --> 00:01:37.560
Betty Friedan wrote
The Feminine Mystique in 1963,

00:01:37.640 --> 00:01:40.200
and in the introduction
describes it as,

00:01:40.280 --> 00:01:42.200
"The problem that has no name."

00:01:42.280 --> 00:01:45.920
It was a book writing about
the experience and aspirations

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:49.520
of women in suburban America.

00:01:49.600 --> 00:01:53.760
It stood out as a book at this time
because the experience of women

00:01:53.840 --> 00:01:57.920
wasn't being written about in
this way for the general population.

00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:01.080
It stood out because she was
standing up and saying,

00:02:01.160 --> 00:02:05.160
"We need to acknowledge that
there is more to life for these women

00:02:05.240 --> 00:02:08.200
than being the perfect wife,
the perfect mother."

00:02:08.280 --> 00:02:13.200
She developed the idea that women
needed to become politicised,

00:02:13.280 --> 00:02:16.680
they needed to think about their
experience at home.

00:02:16.760 --> 00:02:19.120
However,
and there is a big however,

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:23.680
the biggest criticism came from her
exclusion of women of colour.

00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:27.720
Poor women's experience was excluded,
she also excluded lesbians.

00:02:27.800 --> 00:02:29.560
Despite these shortcomings,

00:02:29.640 --> 00:02:32.360
this book had real impact
at the time.

00:02:32.440 --> 00:02:37.560
It bought feminism to a very wide
audience and that's a huge positive.

00:02:37.760 --> 00:02:40.920
Bill McKibben's 1989 book,
The End of Nature,

00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:44.000
is considered to be the first book
to bring global warming

00:02:44.080 --> 00:02:45.600
to a general audience.

00:02:45.680 --> 00:02:50.080
The End of Nature is about how humans
are changing the planet.

00:02:50.160 --> 00:02:53.600
It's a sad lament
on human destruction.

00:02:53.680 --> 00:02:57.120
In the 1980s, people had been
talking about global warming,

00:02:57.200 --> 00:02:59.960
but it hadn't entered the public
consciousness as something

00:03:00.040 --> 00:03:03.000
that we should be
really concerned about,

00:03:03.080 --> 00:03:05.200
and we should be already acting on.

00:03:05.280 --> 00:03:09.640
What he did with this book is really
wake people up

00:03:09.720 --> 00:03:15.720
to this dramatic change that we are
having globally on the natural world.

00:03:16.160 --> 00:03:19.640
And which now is taken for granted
by everybody,

00:03:19.720 --> 00:03:22.840
we all are very aware
of the impact we're having.

00:03:22.920 --> 00:03:24.960
And it's largely due to books
like this.

00:03:25.240 --> 00:03:28.800
Things Fall Apart by Nigerian
author Chinua Achebe,

00:03:28.880 --> 00:03:31.320
was one of the first
African novels in English

00:03:31.400 --> 00:03:33.680
to gain global recognition
and acclaim,

00:03:33.760 --> 00:03:37.560
and it is still read and studied
all over the world to this day.

00:03:37.640 --> 00:03:42.000
Africa was always seen through
the eyes of the white colonials.

00:03:42.520 --> 00:03:43.920
It was like a door opening

00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:47.480
into a world
that white Europeans never understood

00:03:47.560 --> 00:03:50.960
and even as it was published

00:03:51.040 --> 00:03:54.240
people were astonished
by the beauty of the book.

00:03:54.320 --> 00:03:58.600
Here comes a man who's talking about
Igbo villages in Nigeria

00:03:58.880 --> 00:04:02.320
and their lives
and their inner thoughts

00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:05.760
and their hopes and desires
and we'd never seen that.

00:04:05.840 --> 00:04:09.480
And for Africans -
and that's the more important point -

00:04:10.320 --> 00:04:12.520
there were emerging writers,

00:04:12.600 --> 00:04:15.080
they knew their time was now,

00:04:15.160 --> 00:04:17.840
they knew that the world was ready

00:04:17.920 --> 00:04:21.160
and it was that book
that convinced them that it was.

00:04:21.240 --> 00:04:23.840
And finally, to post-war Britain,&lt;/b&gt;

00:04:23.920 --> 00:04:26.320
a time when many items
were still rationed,

00:04:26.400 --> 00:04:30.320
and to Elizabeth David's
A Book of Mediterranean Food.

00:04:30.400 --> 00:04:33.200
Britain already had
a reputation for poor food,

00:04:33.280 --> 00:04:35.920
plain food, grey food.

00:04:36.120 --> 00:04:41.280
She wrote it as a memoir almost,
of her time spent in southern Europe

00:04:41.360 --> 00:04:43.440
before and during World War Two.

00:04:43.520 --> 00:04:47.360
She wrote it really as a yearning
for these wonderful places

00:04:47.440 --> 00:04:50.680
and the colours and the scents
and the flavours.

00:04:50.760 --> 00:04:55.840
These days we completely take for
granted ingredients like chickpeas,

00:04:55.920 --> 00:05:00.400
lemon, garlic,
olive oil, tomatoes, basil.

00:05:00.480 --> 00:05:02.240
But then,
when this book was published,

00:05:02.320 --> 00:05:04.840
none of those ingredients
were really available.

00:05:04.920 --> 00:05:06.200
I mean, if you wanted
to buy olive oil,

00:05:06.280 --> 00:05:07.880
you had to go
to a chemist's shop

00:05:07.960 --> 00:05:10.080
because it was sold
for treating earache.

00:05:10.160 --> 00:05:12.840
She triggered a movement
which we still feel today

00:05:12.920 --> 00:05:16.360
which is one where food
becomes something important

00:05:16.440 --> 00:05:19.200
not just to our daily routine
of nourishing ourselves

00:05:19.280 --> 00:05:21.160
but saying a little bit more
about who we are.

00:05:21.240 --> 00:05:23.360
So there's now multiple generations

00:05:23.440 --> 00:05:27.160
of food writers
and cookery presenters

00:05:27.240 --> 00:05:30.800
whose existence now would not have
been possible without that book.

00:05:31.240 --> 00:05:34.320
Words on a page can do
so much more than entertain.

00:05:34.800 --> 00:05:38.160
Some books have imagined,
explored and introduced ideas

00:05:38.240 --> 00:05:40.840
which have had a lasting impact
on our society

00:05:40.920 --> 00:05:45.400
which we experience to this day,
even if we haven't read them.

